description:
Site: crate-jmap-chat-server/src/message.rs:620-651 (handle_message_set update branch — reaction patch validation).

Current code:

    let mut decoded: Vec<(String, String)> = Vec::with_capacity(reaction_pointer_keys.len());
    let mut bad_desc: Option<String> = None;
    for raw_key in &reaction_pointer_keys {
        let suffix = &raw_key["reactions/".len()..];
        if suffix.is_empty() || suffix.contains('/') || suffix.contains('~') {
            bad_desc = Some(format!(...));
            break;
        }
        let val = augmented.get(raw_key).cloned().unwrap_or(Value::Null);
        if !val.is_null() {
            let ok = val.as_object().is_some_and(|m| ...);
            if !ok {
                bad_desc = Some(format!(...));
                break;
            }
        }
        decoded.push((raw_key.clone(), suffix.to_owned()));
    }
    if let Some(desc) = bad_desc {
        not_updated.insert(id_str, json!({ "type": "invalidPatch", "description": desc }));
        continue;
    }

This is the classic non-idiomatic 'mutable flag + break + post-loop check' pattern that Rust iterator combinators were designed to replace. Three idiomatic rewrites exist:

1. .map().collect::<Result<Vec<_>, String>>() — collects (raw_key, suffix) pairs OR short-circuits on first Err:

    let decoded: Result<Vec<(String, String)>, String> = reaction_pointer_keys
        .iter()
        .map(|raw_key| {
            let suffix = &raw_key["reactions/".len()..];
            if suffix.is_empty() || suffix.contains('/') || suffix.contains('~') {
                return Err(format!(...));
            }
            let val = augmented.get(raw_key).unwrap_or(&Value::Null);
            if !val.is_null() && !val.as_object().is_some_and(|m| ...) {
                return Err(format!(...));
            }
            Ok((raw_key.clone(), suffix.to_owned()))
        })
        .collect();
    let decoded = match decoded {
        Ok(d) => d,
        Err(desc) => { not_updated.insert(...); continue; }
    };

2. Validate-then-decode in two passes via .find_map() + .map().collect():

    if let Some(desc) = reaction_pointer_keys.iter().find_map(|raw_key| { /* validate, return Some(err) or None */ }) {
        not_updated.insert(...); continue;
    }
    let decoded: Vec<(String, String)> = reaction_pointer_keys.iter().map(|rk| (rk.clone(), rk["reactions/".len()..].to_owned())).collect();

3. Inline validation into the subsequent transformation loop (lines 708-732) and skip the decoded vec altogether — the suffix is recomputed cheaply from the raw_key.

What goes wrong if left: the loop has TWO error exits and ONE success exit; a future contributor adding a third validation rule (e.g. max suffix length) must remember to .break with bad_desc set, which is easy to forget, and the diagnostic for the new rule will get filed but with no error consumed at the post-loop check unless the contributor copies the bad_desc = ... pattern exactly. The Result-collecting form makes 'one Err short-circuits' a structural property of the API, not a hand-coded invariant.

This is the same anti-pattern as a Go-style 'errored := false; for { if ... { errored = true; break } }' — exactly what Rust iterators replace.

Scope: P1 iterators (real Rust idiom, not bikeshed).
